A||||15||OverDrive||Fiero Sterotypes?!||||5||
Z||000000||OverDrive||06-21-2001||07:06 PM||||I'm in the process of getting work done to my 84' Convertible. I was talking to a friend of mine, and he said that and I quote "Fiero's are nothing but trouble, you'll be pouring money into it left, right and centre. . . "<BR>I know I'm going to get a bias return on this question, but is this true? I'm not much of a gear head, (electronics freak though), and I don't know how to do much work on cars. Thus, I'd have to pay someone to fix it. Pay them money that I DONT have.<BR>Is there anything I should know before I pour more money into this car?||||reg||5
Z||000001||stsmithgt||06-21-2001||07:12 PM||||YES! Learn how to make more money, because you're pouring money into it left, right, and center!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/laugh2.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Songman||06-21-2001||07:27 PM||||A Fiero is a car, plain and simple. Each one is different. Do cars built in the 80s cost money to maintain? Sure, they do. All of them do. And a lot of Fieros were not taken good care of back then so that adds to it too.<P>Of course, early Fieros had some major points that were addressed in manufacturer recalls. Of course, those were done at the owner's option. So still, each Fiero is different.<P>In my opinion, a Fiero is a very low-budget car to own and drive. If you want to go into all the upgrading stuff that we talk about here you can sink a lot of money into one. If you do, do it because you love it because you will never get it back out of it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||06-21-2001||07:34 PM||||Well, you can get Alpine, Clarion, Pioneer... huh? oh, that's not what you meant..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>In terms of mechanical repair, 84s can potentially be expensive.. they had more problems than other years, especially with the engines. Make sure all the recalls were done, and the engine is in good running order. other than that, you shouldn't have any more problems than with any other mid 80s GM car.<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||06-21-2001||08:23 PM||||My 85 SE daily driver cost me $700.00,<BR>yes it IS a V6, and other than: oil changes,<BR>spark plugs, and a rear caliper that was partially locked up I havn't had to spend one dime on it! <BR>I agree 100% with songman though about the upgrades! My GT NEVER gives me any trouble but I really wouldn't want to look through all my upgrade receipts and add them up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>The Fiero has a (undeserved) bad reputation<BR>and if you listen to everyone else you'll never get one. <BR>"Damn the torpedos! Full speed ahead!"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000005||spideyfoot||06-21-2001||08:25 PM||||You will probably have to do some work on it. But it is a good car to learn with. I reccomend a motorist plan like triple a and a cell phone. A bike rack on the back with a human powered support vehicle. <p>[This message has been edited by spideyfoot (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroHarry||06-21-2001||09:21 PM||||Used cars are used cars.<BR>The money you put in to them is comparable to the amount of stress placed on the car by its owners.<BR>When I say stress I mean how hard it was driven or beaten etc etc.<BR>Any used car is going to require an invesment of some sort to <BR>A: Make it the way you want it (lots of money)<BR>B: Keep it running (again how was it abused before?)<P>Ive found the enjoyment I recieve from my 85GT comparable to the amount ive put into her.<BR>Mine ive had for 3.5 years.<BR>Invested maybe $3,000 in upgrades and about $2,000 in repairs.<BR>In that time i have put over 130K miles on her, SMILING ALL THE WAY!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 266K+ <I>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA</I><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||06-21-2001||09:32 PM||||Anything more than 10 years old is gonna cost ya a bundle if it wasn't kept up properly. 10 years is the cut off time for most US car manufacturers parts support. If you really like the car it's worth any amount to fix the car and do the work right. ||||reg||14
Z||000008||fyerstarter||06-21-2001||10:42 PM||||Well I've only owned two Fieros and both have been very problematic. The first one an 87 GT was pretty old and beatup, not taken very well care of by the previous owner. So I bought the second one, an 86 GT with only 70 original miles on it, in beautifull condition, looks like a brand new car, rebuilt engine only 20,000 miles ago. I have all receipts. Ran excellent all the way home (about 400 miles) and then for about two weeks after that. Then BLAM! The nightmare begins again. I have since spent $1000 in repairs and it is running, but still not right. Getting a check engine light and I have to take it back to my mechanic tommorow for the 3rd time! I have owned this car for less than a month!<P>I love my Fieros but I am actually afraid to drive them! It's not even fun anymore. I can't even listen to the radio for fear I may not hear a problem with the engine. I have been stranded on the side of the road 6 times in the last year in my Fieros. <P>I'm sorry but a bad rap like the Fiero doesn't originate from no-where. There are some people who have had good experiences with them, and there are a lot more that have had bad. It's a shame that a car so good looking and fun to drive have to be such pieces of ****  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Everytime it breaks down I tell myself I'm finished with Fieros, and everytime it's fixed I forget it all over again.<P>If you plan on owning a Fiero as anything more than a weekend car prepare your pocketbook. That's all I can say.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Galen||06-21-2001||11:07 PM||||Fyrstarter, go back to your civic.<P>I agree with what songman said  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> cuz songman is cool.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/kid.gif">         <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000010||PeaceDawg||06-22-2001||12:07 AM||||I am sure every car is different and every Fiero is different too.  This past week I have spent over $1000.00 in repairs on my Fiero.  I have to admit that I am not very mechanical in nature.  If I was I know that I could save a bundle because my car has always been very expensive to maintain and doing the repairs yourself can save you a bundle of cash.  I think that any sporty 2 seater with an engine in the back is gonna be expensive.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000011||Conrad Sanville||06-22-2001||07:58 AM||||There is no such thing as a free ride!<BR>I have been driving Fieros for almost 18 years now and I have had great luck with them. People that know me well know better not to call the cars p.o.s. because my oldest Fiero has outlasted anything they have ever owned.<P>Buying a used car is luck of the draw, I've seen a lot of hack jobs and poorly maintained cars that have obviously been thrashed on.  If you don't have a passion for dealing with an older car go for something newer. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||stsmithgt||06-22-2001||10:00 AM||||If I look at maintainance costs, I have an inexpensive car.  Even with the cost of a new a/c, I average about a hundred dollars a month on repairs.  <BR>The expensive part is when someone runs into you or does more harm than good while they work on it.  I got my car back from a dealership once on fire and once with a hole in the tranny.  They denied, denied and denied.(butwipes..#@!*&...)<BR>Anyway, it's a whole lot cheaper than having the expence of a new car payment, insurance and registration to contend with.  I can get more pleasure out of something that doesn't cost me an arm and a leg so the Fiero is MORE FUN TOO. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||10:44 AM||||OverDrive: <P> there is a point in any cars life cycle where you have to start replacing minor components, and then later, major ones.<P>  it can start as low as 60 to 80k miles, with alternators, starters, catalitic converters, brakes, shocks<P> then around 100k miles you need to start thinking clutch, ball joints, tie rods, suspension bushings, engine mounts<P>  130 to 160 - auto tranny rebuilds, engine rings - valve guides<P>  not to mention things like paint, tires, glass, interior - all wear out depending on how the car is used and cared for.<P>  But the bottom line is: INCOME TAXES!!!<P>  you work at a job for so many hours - they take a big chunk of tax out of that and give you part of the money.<P> If you take your car to someone else to get fixed then you pay sales tax on that, and for the guy you hand the money to that is INCOME - so he pays another big chunk of money out of that as tax, and gets to keep part of it.<P>  SO:<P>  how many hours do you have to work at a job somewhere to pay someone else to work on your car for 1 hour? How much of all the money that changes hands ends up IN your car - and how much goes for TAXES.<BR> <BR>  Figure it out and you will be depressed. remember the guy you pay to work on your car has to pay business taxes too - so HALF of what you hand him may go to the governemt.<P>  The answer: If you spend an hour working on your own car - you pay ZERO taxes on that investment!!! 100% of your time investment goes into your car. Compare that to spending an hour at your regular job and see how much of THAT investment ends up IN your car after everyone else gets their take.<P>  Unless you are a doctor or a lawyer, or make $200/ hr (US dollars) you will be best off if you get a set of craftsmen tools for $40 on sale, and a factory Fiero manual, and start doing everything you can on your car. (borrow the manual from the library at first)<P>  Think of it as a hobbie. At first you will feel like you have no idea what you are doing, and will be afraid of screwing stuff up worse than it already is.<P>  after a few significant repair jobs that you do sucessfully, you will start to wonder "why did I ever pay someone else $70 an hour to do my HOBBIE for me?!"||||reg||1
Z||000014||OverDrive||06-22-2001||04:59 PM||||thats great advice Ken, I may just do that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>thanks a lot everyone!||||reg||1
Z||000015||TRiAD||06-23-2001||08:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fyerstarter:<BR><B>Well I've only owned two Fieros and both have been very problematic. The first one an 87 GT was pretty old and beatup...and the second one, an 86 GT with only 70 original miles on it, in beautifull condition, looks like a brand new car, rebuilt engine only 20,000 miles ago...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1) You made 2 no-nos in car buying (for daily drivers) a) beat up cars, b) no-mile cars. Both situations are equally bad. No-mile cars typically need most bushings, bearings and electronics replaced from sitting. <P>2) You state the 2nd one had "70 original miles", but also that it had a "rebuilt engine only 20,000 miles ago...". <P>What did you mean to say? If it has 70 miles, the engine can't be rebuilt 20Kmi ago. <P>These are true of any car, not just Fieros. If you want a daily driver, but one that has been driven, but maintained. If you buy a beater or a "stored" car, be prepared to restore it to daily duty first. <P>FYI. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
